.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.\" Hand-written: the command is a binary of its own, not part of velarx(1).
.TH VELARX-KUBE 1 "" "velarx" "Velarx"
.SH NAME
velarx\-kube \- play, take down, generate and apply Kubernetes YAML
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B velarx\-kube
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-engine\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-start\-engine ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.br
.B velarx\-kube play
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B velarx\-kube down
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I FILE
.br
.B velarx\-kube generate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR NAME ...
.br
.B velarx\-kube apply
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ NAME ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B velarx\-kube
is run by the user, as
.BR velarx\-api (8)
is: a client of the engine service
.RB ( velarx (1)
.IR varlink ),
which it calls over the typed interface
.IR org.velarx.Engine .
It starts an engine when none serves its
socket, and holds it while it runs.  It links no code of the engine, and the
engine has no command of it.
.PP
It reads what it needs (the YAML, the kubeconfig, the credentials, the secrets
of the engine the YAML refers to), then confines itself and does the work: see
CONFINEMENT.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B play
Create the pods, containers, volumes and secrets the YAML files describe, and
start the pods.  The files, or the \fBhttp://\fR and \fBhttps://\fR addresses, are read in order (\fB\-\fR is the standard input),
the documents of the ConfigMap files of
.B \-\-configmap
last.  The documents of a kind that is not played (a Service, a StatefulSet...)
are skipped.  The report lists the volumes and secrets made, then each pod and its
containers.
.TP
.B down
Remove the pods of the YAML and the secrets its documents made (with
.BR \-\-force ,
its volumes too), and print what was removed.
.TP
.B generate
Write the YAML of pods, containers and volumes of the engine.
.TP
.B apply
Create the YAML of a file, or of containers, pods and volumes (as
.BR generate ),
in the Kubernetes cluster of a kubeconfig, through its API server.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-config " PATH
The configuration file of the engine this command starts when none answers.
.TP
.BI "\-\-engine\-socket " PATH
The socket of the engine service (default:
.I engine/engine.sock
in the runtime directory of the user).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-start\-engine
Do not start an engine when none answers (something else does: the socket
unit of the engine).
.TP
.BI "\-\-log\-level " LEVEL ", \-\-log\-format " FORMAT
The options of
.BR velarx (1)
that
.B velarx kube
passes on to every program it runs.  The warnings and errors of this command
are lines of text on its standard error whatever they say.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS OF PLAY
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Remove the pods of the YAML that exist, then make them.
.TP
.BR \-\-start [ =false ]
Start the pods (\fB\-\-start=false\fR only creates them; init containers need
the start).
.TP
.BI "\-\-network " NAME
The networks of the pods (comma separated or repeated).
.TP
.BR \-\-tls\-verify [ =false ]
Verify the TLS certificates of the registries.
.TP
.BI "\-\-log\-driver " DRIVER ", \-\-log\-opt " KEY=VALUE
The log driver of the containers and its options.
.TP
.BI "\-\-userns " MODE
The user namespace of the pods.
.TP
.BI "\-\-annotation " KEY=VALUE
Annotations of the containers.
.TP
.BI "\-\-publish " [[IP:]HOSTPORT:]CONTAINERPORT[/PROTO]
Publish a port of the pods, over the YAML's.
.TP
.B \-\-publish\-all
Publish every containerPort of the YAML on the same port of the host.
.TP
.BI "\-\-configmap " FILE
YAML files of ConfigMaps the pods use (comma separated or repeated).
.TP
.BI "\-\-ip " ADDRESS ", \-\-mac\-address " ADDRESS
Static addresses of the pods, the n\-th to the n\-th pod (comma separated or
repeated); the pods must be on one bridge network.
.TP
.BR \-\-build [ =false ]
Build the images of the YAML that have a directory of their name with a
Containerfile in the context directory (by default those that are not in the
store).
.TP
.BI "\-\-context\-dir " DIR
The directory of the build directories (default: the current one); needs
.BR \-\-build .
.TP
.B \-\-wait
Stay until the pods exit or a SIGINT or SIGTERM comes, then remove what the
YAML made (volumes too).
.TP
.BR \-\-service\-container [ =true ]
Stay while the pods run, as the main process of a systemd unit: tell systemd
(\fBNOTIFY_SOCKET\fR) when they run, stop them when the unit stops, and exit
when all their containers did.
.TP
.BI "\-\-service\-exit\-code\-propagation " none|all|any
With \fB\-\-service\-container\fR, the exit status: 0 (\fBnone\fR), that of a
container when all of them failed (\fBall\fR) or when one did (\fBany\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-hosts, \-\-no\-hostname
The containers keep the /etc/hosts and the /etc/hostname of their image.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pod\-prefix
Name a container NAME, not POD\-NAME.
.TP
.BI "\-\-seccomp\-profile\-root " DIR
The directory of the \fBLocalhost\fR seccomp profiles (default
/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp).
.TP
.BI "\-\-creds " USER[:PASSWORD] ", \-\-authfile " FILE ", \-\-cert\-dir " DIR
How the pulls and the builds authenticate to the registry.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Accepted; a play prints nothing of the pulls.
.PP
The environment variable \fBPODMAN_SYSTEMD_UNIT\fR names the unit that runs
the play: a label of its containers, which
.B auto\-update
reads.
.SH OPTIONS OF GENERATE
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-service
Add a Service of the published ports.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-type " pod|deployment|daemonset|job
The kind of the workload (default pod).
.TP
.BI "\-r, \-\-replicas " N
The replicas of a Deployment.
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-filename " FILE
Write to this file instead of the standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-podman\-only
Add podman's own annotations of the containers, which Kubernetes does not read.
.SH OPTIONS OF APPLY
.TP
.BI "\-k, \-\-kubeconfig " FILE
The kubeconfig of the cluster (default: $KUBECONFIG).  Its first cluster and
user are used.
.TP
.BI "\-\-ca\-cert\-file " FILE|insecure
The CA of the API server, or \fBinsecure\fR to skip its verification.
.TP
.BI "\-\-ns " NAMESPACE
The namespace (default: default).
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-file " FILE
The YAML file or http(s) address, or \- for the standard input; without it, the names are
containers, pods or volumes of the engine whose YAML is made first.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-service
With names: add a Service of the published ports.
.SH CONFINEMENT
Once it has read its inputs the process gives up what it does not need, with
every right of Landlock ABI 9 a must (a kernel that cannot enforce them does
not run it) and a seccomp filter: it reads no file; it makes files and
directories only beneath the nearest existing directory of the host paths
Kubernetes creates (\fBDirectoryOrCreate\fR, \fBFileOrCreate\fR) and of the
file of \fBgenerate \-\-filename\fR; it looks up UNIX sockets only beneath the
directory of the engine's socket (and of \fBNOTIFY_SOCKET\fR); it connects to
no TCP port, except, for \fBapply\fR, the port of the API server of the
kubeconfig; it executes nothing, makes no process, and signals nothing outside
itself.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 125 for an error (the message is on the standard error).  With
\fB\-\-service\-container\fR, the status of \fB\-\-service\-exit\-code\-propagation\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velarx (1),
.BR velarx\-api (8),
.BR velarx.conf (5)
